  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1241 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nPleasant weather remains through Wednesday night as the region of\ndry air will continue to provide cooler, refreshing mornings and \nmostly sunny but warm afternoons. A north-northeasterly flow is \nexpected to maintain through midweek but should trend lighter \ncompared to speeds seen this afternoon and on Monday.\n\nA broad mid-level disturbance establishing later this week across\nWest Texas through the Concho Valley may be enough to be able to \nhelp squeeze out some low end rain chances (~15-25%) late this \nweek across the region. Chances peak late Thursday through Friday.\nThis mid-level disturbance may be aided by some influence and \nmoisture from the development of a potential surface low in the \nBay of Campeche. The National Hurricane Center is now monitoring \nthat potential across the Bay of Campeche and has provided up to a\nmedium (40%) chance of development into a Tropical Depression or \nStorm over the next 7 days. Nonetheless, the steering flow favors \nthat if a surface low is able to develop, it would suggest a \nnorth-northeasterly trajectory with time. This would put most of \nTexas, including our region, on the dry side of the low. This may \nhelp to dry out our forecast through the weekend. A caveat with \nbeing on the dry side of any potential system would be that the \ntemperatures will warm up easier where afternoon highs in the \nregion by Sunday could climb into the low 90s for many. A couple \nlocations may even potentially get back into the mid 90s.",
